00:01:39.560 --> 00:01:42.890
OK, so let's start with a little
bit of an examination of what

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:45.210
do you need from a data center.

00:01:45.210 --> 00:01:48.410
Well, if you're like me, and
I suspect most of you are,

00:01:48.410 --> 00:01:53.900
you need your data center
to be very high performance.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:56.960
You need it to be
highly available.

00:01:56.960 --> 00:02:01.330
You need it in places
where you need to be.

00:02:01.330 --> 00:02:03.725
You need it to be
extremely reliable.

00:02:03.725 --> 00:02:06.840
It needs to be ultra secure.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:12.050
And if you can wrap that up
with a bow of sustainability,

00:02:12.050 --> 00:02:14.780
then you've got something that
is truly meeting all your needs

00:02:14.780 --> 00:02:17.920
in a data center.

00:02:17.920 --> 00:02:20.680
Since the earliest days
of when I got to Google,

00:02:20.680 --> 00:02:23.410
and actually long
before I was there

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:25.690
for about two
decades now, Google

00:02:25.690 --> 00:02:28.000
has been the leaders in
ultra-high performance

00:02:28.000 --> 00:02:30.730
computing systems.

00:02:30.730 --> 00:02:34.780
One of the things that impressed
me when I first arrived

00:02:34.780 --> 00:02:37.930
was that, even as innovative
as the data centers were,

00:02:37.930 --> 00:02:40.890
I remember the very first day
that I was at a Google data

00:02:40.890 --> 00:02:43.420
center, and everyone
wonders what a data

00:02:43.420 --> 00:02:44.950
center is like at Google.

00:02:44.950 --> 00:02:46.180
I did too.

00:02:46.180 --> 00:02:48.610
When I got there, I
was tracing the path

00:02:48.610 --> 00:02:51.877
of the electrons in my mind
and I was just shaking my head.

00:02:51.877 --> 00:02:53.710
And my manager asked
me, is something wrong.

00:02:53.710 --> 00:02:56.620
And I said, no, it's just
so obvious I don't know

00:02:56.620 --> 00:02:59.120
why everyone isn't doing it.

00:02:59.120 --> 00:03:01.150
But what impressed
me most is that

00:03:01.150 --> 00:03:04.150
no matter how good
we're doing, we always

00:03:04.150 --> 00:03:05.510
believe we can do better.

00:03:05.510 --> 00:03:07.960
So we have a relentless
focus on innovation

00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:10.330
and constant
technology evolution.

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:18.180
So ultra-high performance,
what does that mean?

00:03:18.180 --> 00:03:22.040
Well, for us, it means
that we don't rely on just

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:24.440
off-the-shelf components.

00:03:24.440 --> 00:03:28.280
We design and build almost
every part of the stack,

00:03:28.280 --> 00:03:32.270
custom built for us for
ultra-high computing ultra-high

00:03:32.270 --> 00:03:37.340
efficiency, at the
price and performance

00:03:37.340 --> 00:03:38.870
that we need it to be.

00:03:38.870 --> 00:03:42.740
So this is everything from our
custom designed, high voltage

00:03:42.740 --> 00:03:46.010
substations that are on the
campus of all of our data

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:49.670
centers, to the
servers themselves,

00:03:49.670 --> 00:03:51.860
the racks that
the servers go in,

00:03:51.860 --> 00:03:54.500
the custom designed
cooling solutions

00:03:54.500 --> 00:03:58.160
that those racks mate
to, to even the way we

00:03:58.160 --> 00:03:59.548
operate the cooling plants.

00:03:59.548 --> 00:04:01.090
I'll touch on that
a little bit more.

00:04:01.090 --> 00:04:03.860
But as I mentioned,
it truly is just

00:04:03.860 --> 00:04:07.610
a relentless focus on
continual improvement

00:04:07.610 --> 00:04:11.770
and constant innovation.

00:04:11.770 --> 00:04:14.440
So as I mentioned, these
are built for Google.

00:04:14.440 --> 00:04:17.339
There's no unnecessary
components in our servers.

00:04:17.339 --> 00:04:20.100
They're stripped down,
just ultra-high-performance

00:04:20.100 --> 00:04:22.019
computers.

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:23.730
This reduces vulnerabilities.

00:04:23.730 --> 00:04:30.190
As you know, we have a very,
very strong focus on security.

00:04:30.190 --> 00:04:32.910
So all of our servers have
our built-in Titan chip,

00:04:32.910 --> 00:04:34.620
from the time that
they're manufactured

00:04:34.620 --> 00:04:37.130
for us to our specifications.

00:04:37.130 --> 00:04:40.560
And that Titan chip is
helping to secure every server

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:44.160
throughout its entire
lifecycle at Google.

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:47.550
All of our services are
dynamically allocated.

00:04:47.550 --> 00:04:49.410
We talk a lot about this.

00:04:49.410 --> 00:04:52.440
Our Borg system, which we
have been using internally

00:04:52.440 --> 00:04:54.420
at Google since
the earliest days,

00:04:54.420 --> 00:04:57.300
that eventually became
the orchestration layer

00:04:57.300 --> 00:05:01.120
for all of our containers.

00:05:01.120 --> 00:05:06.510
And so we are constantly
provisioning and upgrading

00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.870
and redoing our infrastructure.

00:05:09.870 --> 00:05:12.670
And since we're constantly
upgrading and changing,

00:05:12.670 --> 00:05:15.510
it has to be very
flexible and adaptable.

00:05:15.510 --> 00:05:19.980
So we put a high
priority on optionality.

00:05:19.980 --> 00:05:22.560
I'll talk a little bit
more of what that means.

00:05:22.560 --> 00:05:26.290
Has to be extremely
efficient, ability to scale.

00:05:26.290 --> 00:05:30.930
And our data centers also are
compliant with the Open Compute

00:05:30.930 --> 00:05:33.750
Program, the racks that
are available through that.

00:05:33.750 --> 00:05:35.710
So why is that important?

00:05:35.710 --> 00:05:38.620
Well, it just means that we're
leveraging the rest of the data

00:05:38.620 --> 00:05:42.270
center ecosystem as well, not
just relying on what we do.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:52.580
We also have our own teams that
are staffing the data centers,

00:05:52.580 --> 00:05:55.610
in particular our
hardware operations teams

00:05:55.610 --> 00:05:58.790
that are there 24/7
at our data centers.

00:05:58.790 --> 00:06:02.360
They're doing all
of our deployments,

00:06:02.360 --> 00:06:06.180
maintenance, upgrades, repairs
of all of the hardware,

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:09.450
all the servers, storage, and
network in the data centers.

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:13.020
They're there in real time,
so they respond very quickly.

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:15.290
They have all the
tools necessary to make

00:06:15.290 --> 00:06:17.090
their job very efficient.

00:06:17.090 --> 00:06:22.410
And they are highly reliable
and do this all the time.

00:06:22.410 --> 00:06:25.670
So we're not relying on an
SLA with a service provider

00:06:25.670 --> 00:06:27.710
to come in and service
the data centers.

00:06:27.710 --> 00:06:31.850
We do that ourselves.

00:06:31.850 --> 00:06:34.580
Since I've been at
Google, we have constantly

00:06:34.580 --> 00:06:36.470
been innovating in cooling.

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:37.560
Why is that important?

00:06:37.560 --> 00:06:41.610
Well, data centers
consume a lot of energy.

00:06:41.610 --> 00:06:45.337
And in a data center, one of
the largest overhead components

00:06:45.337 --> 00:06:47.420
is related to the air
conditioning, or the cooling

00:06:47.420 --> 00:06:48.770
of the data center.

00:06:48.770 --> 00:06:51.200
So we have innovated
our cooling systems

00:06:51.200 --> 00:06:55.130
about every 18 months since the
time that I've been at Google.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:59.360
We've used a variety
of cooling techniques.

00:06:59.360 --> 00:07:00.680
We use seawater.

00:07:00.680 --> 00:07:03.020
In Finland, we bought
a paper mill, turned it

00:07:03.020 --> 00:07:05.000
into a big data center campus.

00:07:05.000 --> 00:07:08.078
And it actually was sitting
right on the Gulf of Finland.

00:07:08.078 --> 00:07:09.620
So we were looking
at that water that

00:07:09.620 --> 00:07:13.550
was about 14 degrees Celsius
all year round and said,

00:07:13.550 --> 00:07:14.900
we can use that.

00:07:14.900 --> 00:07:18.620
So that data center is
100% cooled by seawater.

00:07:18.620 --> 00:07:22.130
We use reclaimed and
stormwater in a data center

00:07:22.130 --> 00:07:24.020
in the southeast of
the United States.

00:07:24.020 --> 00:07:27.500
We built a stormwater
capture and reuse system.

00:07:27.500 --> 00:07:30.620
So we're cooling the data
center with stormwater there.

00:07:30.620 --> 00:07:33.050
In Belgium, were
sited right next

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:35.480
to a big industrial
shipping canal.

00:07:35.480 --> 00:07:39.120
So we used the industrial
shipping canal water.

00:07:39.120 --> 00:07:41.120
The point is, you see
where I'm going with this.

00:07:41.120 --> 00:07:43.370
We don't have a
cookie cutter design.

00:07:43.370 --> 00:07:46.010
We don't have a
one-size-fits-all model.

00:07:46.010 --> 00:07:48.560
We optimize the designs
of the data center

00:07:48.560 --> 00:07:51.590
for that specific location,
for the environment

00:07:51.590 --> 00:07:54.020
that it's working
in to make most

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:56.240
use of the natural
resources that are there.

00:08:00.860 --> 00:08:04.490
So that's what you need
from a data center.

00:08:04.490 --> 00:08:07.388
But you also have to consider
where the data centers are.

00:08:07.388 --> 00:08:08.930
You need them in
the right locations.

00:08:08.930 --> 00:08:13.950
They have to meet you
where you need to be.

00:08:13.950 --> 00:08:20.570
So we have a very large
footprint of data centers.

00:08:20.570 --> 00:08:23.390
We have 16 global campuses.

00:08:23.390 --> 00:08:26.960
When I talk about a
campus, a campus to us

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.010
is typically hundreds of
acres with many buildings,

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:35.370
many data center buildings
on each campus, all connected

00:08:35.370 --> 00:08:39.870
by one of the world's largest
most high performance public

00:08:39.870 --> 00:08:42.330
networks available.

00:08:42.330 --> 00:08:46.470
We have 19 regions
for our cloud compute.

00:08:46.470 --> 00:08:49.730
We have 58 availability
zones there.

00:08:49.730 --> 00:08:52.920
134 points of presence.

00:08:52.920 --> 00:08:57.900
We are in over 200
countries around the world,

00:08:57.900 --> 00:09:02.100
all interconnected with hundreds
of thousands of miles of fiber,

00:09:02.100 --> 00:09:06.150
including 13 of our
own sub-sea cables,

00:09:06.150 --> 00:09:12.220
making us really the largest
overall compute infrastructure

00:09:12.220 --> 00:09:15.190
of any of the major players.

00:09:22.330 --> 00:09:26.740
So even long before
cloud computing

00:09:26.740 --> 00:09:29.260
was a large business
for us, we had

00:09:29.260 --> 00:09:31.570
been building this
infrastructure for a couple

00:09:31.570 --> 00:09:33.550
decades to service
our own needs,

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:39.490
to service Gmail and search
and ads and all of the YouTube

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:41.690
and all of the Google services.

00:09:41.690 --> 00:09:44.470
So we have been building
this infrastructure.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:46.000
And we learned how
to scale and we

00:09:46.000 --> 00:09:50.530
learned how to build efficiently
for our own internal needs.

00:09:50.530 --> 00:09:53.410
What that means is that we
have eight products currently,

00:09:53.410 --> 00:09:59.158
that each are serving over a
billion active customers a day.

00:09:59.158 --> 00:10:00.950
It's a lot of learning
that came with that.

00:10:00.950 --> 00:10:04.780
And all of that gets
applied to Google Cloud.

00:10:04.780 --> 00:10:07.000
So they're benefiting
from two decades

00:10:07.000 --> 00:10:09.730
of learning how to
scale, how to build,

00:10:09.730 --> 00:10:12.081
how to do things
hyper-efficiently.

00:10:17.160 --> 00:10:19.050
So where are our data centers?

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:23.850
Well, we have data center
campuses on four continents--

00:10:23.850 --> 00:10:27.630
North America, South America,
Asia, and Europe, of course.

00:10:27.630 --> 00:10:31.620
We pick the sites based on
a rigorous time-tested site

00:10:31.620 --> 00:10:34.300
selection criteria.

00:10:34.300 --> 00:10:36.880
We take input from our
internal customers.

00:10:36.880 --> 00:10:39.090
Cloud is one of our
largest customers

00:10:39.090 --> 00:10:42.270
now, dictating a
lot of where they

00:10:42.270 --> 00:10:44.130
want to be to
serve the customers

00:10:44.130 --> 00:10:46.090
and meet you where you are.

00:10:46.090 --> 00:10:51.150
So with that, we look for things
that we can more or less build

00:10:51.150 --> 00:10:54.130
into for the next decade or so.

00:10:54.130 --> 00:10:56.250
So our strategy is that
we want to find sites

00:10:56.250 --> 00:11:00.630
that we can be building at and
building up for about 10 years

00:11:00.630 --> 00:11:02.670
or more.

00:11:02.670 --> 00:11:04.680
There is a long
list of criteria.

00:11:04.680 --> 00:11:07.500
A lot of people always ask me,
how do you choose a data center

00:11:07.500 --> 00:11:08.410
site.

00:11:08.410 --> 00:11:12.450
And if I were to quote the head
of our site selection team,

00:11:12.450 --> 00:11:15.780
he would probably say, it's more
or less the art of compromise.

00:11:15.780 --> 00:11:17.520
Because there is
no one site that

00:11:17.520 --> 00:11:20.880
meets 100% of every single
thing we would love,

00:11:20.880 --> 00:11:23.640
so we try and find
the best compromise.

00:11:23.640 --> 00:11:25.320
But what that means is that--

00:11:25.320 --> 00:11:27.610
like this site here
is in Belgium--

00:11:27.610 --> 00:11:30.120
we wind up with a
large parcel of land

00:11:30.120 --> 00:11:32.850
that we can build multiple
data centers on for many years

00:11:32.850 --> 00:11:34.660
to come.

00:11:34.660 --> 00:11:39.630
And that gives us
access to our customers,

00:11:39.630 --> 00:11:43.740
gives us access to a
workforce, gives us

00:11:43.740 --> 00:11:48.700
access to high quality
fiber, and, more importantly,

00:11:48.700 --> 00:11:51.210
every day gives us access
to renewable energy.

00:11:56.490 --> 00:11:58.950
This particular data
center is in Iowa.

00:11:58.950 --> 00:12:01.230
This is in our central region.

00:12:01.230 --> 00:12:05.160
This is the first part of
the parcel that we developed.

00:12:05.160 --> 00:12:08.040
This part of the
parcel is 300 acres.

00:12:08.040 --> 00:12:12.920
The overall site is about
three times that size.

00:12:12.920 --> 00:12:16.820
If you were to
measure this out, this

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:20.450
fits about 235 American
football fields

00:12:20.450 --> 00:12:23.840
on this part that's
the developed part.

00:12:23.840 --> 00:12:26.450
We have another two parcels
that we could develop

00:12:26.450 --> 00:12:29.240
that are equally this large.

00:12:29.240 --> 00:12:35.460
You'll see in the foreground
here that that small building?

00:12:35.460 --> 00:12:39.320
That's a one-story data center,
that building is about a third

00:12:39.320 --> 00:12:41.220
of a mile long.

00:12:41.220 --> 00:12:43.250
And then behind it,
you see the data center

00:12:43.250 --> 00:12:44.700
that's a little taller?

00:12:44.700 --> 00:12:48.415
That's a two-story data center,
also a third of a mile long.

00:12:48.415 --> 00:12:50.040
And then the ones
that you see that are

00:12:50.040 --> 00:12:51.960
being built behind that?

00:12:51.960 --> 00:12:54.270
Those are four-story
data centers.

00:12:54.270 --> 00:12:59.100
Also will be about a third of
a mile long when completed.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:03.070
The amount of capacity
here is staggering.

00:13:03.070 --> 00:13:06.420
And it provides a good exercise
opportunity for our teams

00:13:06.420 --> 00:13:07.387
that are at the site.

00:13:07.387 --> 00:13:08.970
They can just jog
around the perimeter

00:13:08.970 --> 00:13:11.158
and do about almost three
miles of jogging just

00:13:11.158 --> 00:13:12.700
to go around the
buildings right now.

00:13:17.570 --> 00:13:19.600
So you've got the
what you need, you've

00:13:19.600 --> 00:13:24.160
got the where you need
it, but what about how?

00:13:24.160 --> 00:13:29.110
Well, you need it
available all the time.

00:13:29.110 --> 00:13:31.690
If you caught the keynote
this morning by Urs,

00:13:31.690 --> 00:13:34.120
he mentioned that
unsolicited by Google,

00:13:34.120 --> 00:13:37.690
not commissioned by us at all,
but an independent industry

00:13:37.690 --> 00:13:40.930
analyst said that,
by far, Google Cloud

00:13:40.930 --> 00:13:44.770
has the lowest downtime of any
of the major cloud providers.

00:13:44.770 --> 00:13:46.125
That's not by accident.

00:13:46.125 --> 00:13:47.750
It's because of the
way that we design,

00:13:47.750 --> 00:13:49.380
build, and operate
our data centers.

00:13:54.410 --> 00:14:01.150
And when we say 7 by 24 by
365, we really mean that.

00:14:01.150 --> 00:14:05.740
The design principle of our data
centers is to be fault tolerant

00:14:05.740 --> 00:14:10.210
and allow us to maintain the
data centers transparent to all

00:14:10.210 --> 00:14:11.090
of you.

00:14:11.090 --> 00:14:13.270
What that means is
that, look, you can

00:14:13.270 --> 00:14:14.540
hope that things don't break.

00:14:14.540 --> 00:14:16.660
But I'm here to tell
you, things break.

00:14:16.660 --> 00:14:18.340
When they do break,
you shouldn't

00:14:18.340 --> 00:14:20.735
care because it should
be transparent to you.

00:14:20.735 --> 00:14:22.360
So that means we have
to do maintenance

00:14:22.360 --> 00:14:25.990
and we have to do upgrades
and repairs while the data

00:14:25.990 --> 00:14:27.310
center is still live.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:32.740
So we build in the ability to,
one, make it fault tolerant,

00:14:32.740 --> 00:14:35.374
and, two, make it
concurrently maintainable.

00:14:39.170 --> 00:14:40.610
We also build in hooks.

00:14:40.610 --> 00:14:46.970
I talked a little bit earlier
about our value of optionality.

00:14:46.970 --> 00:14:49.642
What that means is that we
don't have a crystal ball.

00:14:49.642 --> 00:14:52.100
I don't know exactly what we're
going to need from our data

00:14:52.100 --> 00:14:53.570
center a few years from now.

00:14:53.570 --> 00:14:56.240
I don't know what the latest
generation of platforms that

00:14:56.240 --> 00:14:58.280
are going to be
developed are, but we

00:14:58.280 --> 00:15:01.880
want to add in the hooks
that allow us to accommodate

00:15:01.880 --> 00:15:05.240
for whatever our platform's
development team comes up with.

00:15:05.240 --> 00:15:08.300
One example is when we built
our first data centers,

00:15:08.300 --> 00:15:11.960
we didn't know about the TPU
systems that were developed

00:15:11.960 --> 00:15:16.460
for our ML and AI applications.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:19.400
However, we were able
to accommodate those,

00:15:19.400 --> 00:15:21.680
and even the latest
generation that requires water

00:15:21.680 --> 00:15:24.120
cooling at the chip level.

00:15:24.120 --> 00:15:26.090
We were able to
accommodate that because we

00:15:26.090 --> 00:15:29.390
had built in the hooks to allow
us to do those types of things

00:15:29.390 --> 00:15:30.720
in the future.

00:15:30.720 --> 00:15:32.870
So really what the idea
there is that we're

00:15:32.870 --> 00:15:35.390
looking to the future
and we're planning

00:15:35.390 --> 00:15:38.180
to be able to accommodate
whatever the requirements are.

00:15:42.060 --> 00:15:46.260
And from our operations team,
one of our core principles

00:15:46.260 --> 00:15:49.710
is on continuous learning,
continuous improvement.

00:15:49.710 --> 00:15:52.610
And what that means
is transparency.

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:55.150
If you make a mistake
you can't hide it.

00:15:55.150 --> 00:15:56.560
People make mistakes.

00:15:56.560 --> 00:15:57.640
It's OK.

00:15:57.640 --> 00:16:00.520
We have a no blame policy,
because we just want

00:16:00.520 --> 00:16:02.770
to learn from what happened.

00:16:02.770 --> 00:16:05.380
And it's typical in the
industry that of all the data

00:16:05.380 --> 00:16:09.820
center failures, still about
70% of all data center failures

00:16:09.820 --> 00:16:13.300
today are related to
some form of human error.

00:16:13.300 --> 00:16:15.210
At Google, ours are much less.

00:16:15.210 --> 00:16:17.520
It's a little bit over
15% that are related

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:20.970
to some sort of human error.

00:16:20.970 --> 00:16:23.300
However, because of
our designs, because we

00:16:23.300 --> 00:16:26.570
don't expect perfection,
we design for them

00:16:26.570 --> 00:16:28.775
to be tolerant of mistakes.

00:16:28.775 --> 00:16:30.650
And what that means is
that even though there

00:16:30.650 --> 00:16:34.400
were 15% of the errors that
were human-related, none of them

00:16:34.400 --> 00:16:37.730
resulted in downtime
of the data centers.

00:16:37.730 --> 00:16:41.310
That's also quite different
from the industry average.

00:16:46.010 --> 00:16:49.470
In order to help our teams
manage the data centers,

00:16:49.470 --> 00:16:52.100
we have to monitor everything.

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:55.530
You might say that we're a
little bit data obsessed.

00:16:55.530 --> 00:16:59.510
So we're monitoring every
aspect of the data centers,

00:16:59.510 --> 00:17:04.130
from the servers themselves,
monitoring temperatures and air

00:17:04.130 --> 00:17:05.660
pressures and flow rates.

00:17:05.660 --> 00:17:08.800
And in the mechanical
cooling yard,

00:17:08.800 --> 00:17:12.560
the flow rates and
differential pressures

00:17:12.560 --> 00:17:14.569
of the cooling systems.

00:17:14.569 --> 00:17:16.339
On the electrical
side, everything

00:17:16.339 --> 00:17:21.079
from the high voltage substation
all the way down to the server

00:17:21.079 --> 00:17:22.730
level is monitored.

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:25.400
And of course, security.

00:17:25.400 --> 00:17:28.490
All of our security systems,
all of our multi-layer security

00:17:28.490 --> 00:17:29.415
systems.

00:17:29.415 --> 00:17:30.540
So these are all monitored.

00:17:30.540 --> 00:17:32.480
Truly, we have
world-class monitoring,

00:17:32.480 --> 00:17:35.540
from chip all the
way to chiller.

00:17:35.540 --> 00:17:37.700
And that enables our
teams to understand

00:17:37.700 --> 00:17:40.820
what's going on so that they
can best operate, maintain,

00:17:40.820 --> 00:17:43.310
and repair the data centers.

00:17:47.690 --> 00:17:50.210
Speaking of security,
data centers

00:17:50.210 --> 00:17:52.300
have to be ultra secure.

00:17:52.300 --> 00:17:54.620
And ours are ultra secure.

00:17:57.670 --> 00:17:59.710
There was a recent
CBRE study that

00:17:59.710 --> 00:18:04.600
said that still one of the
highest priorities in site

00:18:04.600 --> 00:18:08.140
selection for where to place
their equipment in a data

00:18:08.140 --> 00:18:12.070
center is based on the security
profile of the data center.

00:18:12.070 --> 00:18:13.670
So we take that very seriously.

00:18:13.670 --> 00:18:18.220
We employ a
defense-in-depth type

00:18:18.220 --> 00:18:21.580
of a strategy for physical
security at the data centers.

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:24.910
That means that from the
very outer perimeter,

00:18:24.910 --> 00:18:28.490
around a campus that's
hundreds of acres large,

00:18:28.490 --> 00:18:31.750
there's already barriers and
fencing to prevent people

00:18:31.750 --> 00:18:33.430
from just wandering in.

00:18:33.430 --> 00:18:36.370
And the closer you get to
the data center itself,

00:18:36.370 --> 00:18:44.020
the higher the security, the
more rigid the policies are.

00:18:44.020 --> 00:18:47.050
And what all this means is
that out of all the data

00:18:47.050 --> 00:18:52.150
centers around the world,
less than 2% of Googlers

00:18:52.150 --> 00:18:55.500
have ever set foot in
one of our data centers.

00:18:55.500 --> 00:19:00.610
And that's done by
design, because protecting

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:06.700
the data and the information
and the privacy of our users

00:19:06.700 --> 00:19:10.160
is at the foremost of our design
criteria for the data center.

00:19:10.160 --> 00:19:14.380
So unless you have a job that
requires you to be at the data

00:19:14.380 --> 00:19:18.443
center to perform that job, you
don't go to the data centers.

00:19:18.443 --> 00:19:19.360
It's just that strict.

00:19:24.630 --> 00:19:27.030
So I talked about our
physical security.

00:19:27.030 --> 00:19:30.240
But truly, physical security
and information security

00:19:30.240 --> 00:19:32.410
have to go hand-in-hand.

00:19:32.410 --> 00:19:37.040
It's part of the
same design criteria.

00:19:37.040 --> 00:19:39.250
However, our information
security team

00:19:39.250 --> 00:19:41.010
are separate from my team.

00:19:41.010 --> 00:19:43.930
And that is done by
design because, one,

00:19:43.930 --> 00:19:46.300
they truly are the
world's experts

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:51.100
in protecting
information security.

00:19:51.100 --> 00:19:52.900
It's a very large team.

00:19:52.900 --> 00:19:55.210
They've been published
and written books

00:19:55.210 --> 00:19:57.310
on security principles
and all that.

00:19:57.310 --> 00:19:59.620
And truly, they need
to be standalone.

00:19:59.620 --> 00:20:01.690
And it's also for
governance because you

00:20:01.690 --> 00:20:04.870
want the two teams
to be little checks

00:20:04.870 --> 00:20:06.070
and balances on each other.

00:20:08.900 --> 00:20:11.270
So as I said on the
physical security side,

00:20:11.270 --> 00:20:14.540
we use a multi-layered
strategy to get

00:20:14.540 --> 00:20:16.770
into the data center itself.

00:20:16.770 --> 00:20:19.770
You have to have a badge that
authorizes you to be there.

00:20:19.770 --> 00:20:22.290
And that's tied to your
credentials, of course.

00:20:22.290 --> 00:20:24.300
Then we have biometric
authentication,

00:20:24.300 --> 00:20:25.927
which is pretty typical.

00:20:25.927 --> 00:20:28.010
But then we have a lot of
other layers of security

00:20:28.010 --> 00:20:29.900
that are not seen.

00:20:29.900 --> 00:20:35.720
So we know who is in the
data centers, where they are.

00:20:35.720 --> 00:20:37.730
We know what they've
touched, why they

00:20:37.730 --> 00:20:39.800
did it, what actions they took.

00:20:39.800 --> 00:20:43.225
So that we have complete
end-to-end traceability

00:20:43.225 --> 00:20:45.350
of everything that's
happening in the data centers.

00:20:52.560 --> 00:20:56.030
This is a quote from
Data Center Knowledge.

00:20:56.030 --> 00:20:58.430
Basically, it's just
summarizing what

00:20:58.430 --> 00:21:01.700
I've been saying is that data
center security measures are

00:21:01.700 --> 00:21:05.270
built into pretty much every
single layer of the system,

00:21:05.270 --> 00:21:08.750
from the data center itself,
the building, the security

00:21:08.750 --> 00:21:11.300
principles, the security
designs, all the way down

00:21:11.300 --> 00:21:12.580
to the servers themselves.

00:21:12.580 --> 00:21:15.200
And our custom security chip
is built into the servers.

00:21:20.930 --> 00:21:27.550
Now we know what you want, what
you need from a data center.

00:21:27.550 --> 00:21:30.970
We know how you need
it, where you need it.

00:21:30.970 --> 00:21:34.067
So we've covered
most of the reasons.

00:21:34.067 --> 00:21:35.650
But what if you could
wrap all this up

00:21:35.650 --> 00:21:37.870
with sustainability also?

00:21:37.870 --> 00:21:40.750
More and more, our
customers are telling us

00:21:40.750 --> 00:21:43.180
that they have their
own corporate mandates

00:21:43.180 --> 00:21:48.760
around sustainability and
energy use and renewable energy

00:21:48.760 --> 00:21:50.750
purchases.

00:21:50.750 --> 00:21:55.510
So we have been focused
on sustainability for long

00:21:55.510 --> 00:21:57.610
before I came to Google.

00:21:57.610 --> 00:22:06.510
We were the first company
to achieve a zero carbon

00:22:06.510 --> 00:22:07.200
footprint.

00:22:07.200 --> 00:22:09.460
And I'll talk a little
bit more about that.

00:22:09.460 --> 00:22:11.970
But really, our
sustainability efforts

00:22:11.970 --> 00:22:15.900
are broken into three
pillars, if you will.

00:22:15.900 --> 00:22:17.550
Energy efficiency.

00:22:17.550 --> 00:22:21.090
Because truly, the
best use of energy

00:22:21.090 --> 00:22:23.380
is the energy you don't
use in the first place.

00:22:23.380 --> 00:22:26.250
So we've been laser focused
on energy efficiency

00:22:26.250 --> 00:22:28.210
at the data centers.

00:22:28.210 --> 00:22:31.870
Second, our use of
renewable energy to power

00:22:31.870 --> 00:22:33.250
the data centers.

00:22:33.250 --> 00:22:35.630
I'll get into a lot
more detail about that.

00:22:35.630 --> 00:22:38.560
And lastly, our commitment
to a circular environment,

00:22:38.560 --> 00:22:41.527
and our philosophy of a
zero waste to landfill.

00:22:41.527 --> 00:22:43.110
And I'll talk more
about that as well.

00:22:45.700 --> 00:22:48.100
So as I said, the
best energy saved

00:22:48.100 --> 00:22:51.910
is the energy not consumed
in the first place.

00:22:51.910 --> 00:22:55.290
Google data centers
are without a doubt

00:22:55.290 --> 00:22:58.360
some of the most
efficient in the world.

00:22:58.360 --> 00:23:01.660
What that means is that compared
to a traditional enterprise

00:23:01.660 --> 00:23:04.810
data center, a Google data
center consumes about half

00:23:04.810 --> 00:23:09.010
of the energy that a traditional
enterprise data center would.

00:23:09.010 --> 00:23:11.950
And at the same time,
we're delivering

00:23:11.950 --> 00:23:15.490
seven times more compute
power at our data centers

00:23:15.490 --> 00:23:19.270
today than we were
just five years ago.

00:23:19.270 --> 00:23:21.240
So what that means
is that that's

00:23:21.240 --> 00:23:24.690
a lot more Gmail, a lot more
search, a lot more YouTube cat

00:23:24.690 --> 00:23:28.710
videos, and a lot bigger
cloud for the same energy

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.930
footprint as just
a few years ago.

00:23:35.980 --> 00:23:39.670
As a matter of fact, one
measure of energy efficiency

00:23:39.670 --> 00:23:43.240
in the data centers is a very
simple metric called PUE.

00:23:43.240 --> 00:23:45.100
Most of you have
probably heard of it,

00:23:45.100 --> 00:23:46.970
it's a very simple ratio.

00:23:46.970 --> 00:23:50.350
It's the total energy consumed
at the data center divided

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:52.570
by the IT energy used.

00:23:52.570 --> 00:23:56.690
And so everything
else is overhead.

00:23:56.690 --> 00:24:01.990
So it's a ratio that generally
measures something above 1.

00:24:01.990 --> 00:24:07.120
And most average data
centers are still about 1.58.

00:24:07.120 --> 00:24:11.360
What that means is that
they have 58% overhead.

00:24:11.360 --> 00:24:14.260
Our data centers
have 11% overhead.

00:24:14.260 --> 00:24:17.200
And this is a trailing
12-month average

00:24:17.200 --> 00:24:20.020
across the fleet of every
single one of our data centers

00:24:20.020 --> 00:24:20.960
around the world.

00:24:20.960 --> 00:24:24.140
This is not cherry picked, this
is not our best data center,

00:24:24.140 --> 00:24:26.260
this is across the entire fleet.

00:24:26.260 --> 00:24:28.630
And it's been that
way for a long time.

00:24:34.220 --> 00:24:37.690
But we didn't just rest on
making energy efficient data

00:24:37.690 --> 00:24:41.500
centers and building energy
efficient computing systems,

00:24:41.500 --> 00:24:44.830
we then decided to try and
make better use of the data

00:24:44.830 --> 00:24:47.110
that we're getting
from our data centers.

00:24:47.110 --> 00:24:49.150
One of our engineers
came up with the idea

00:24:49.150 --> 00:24:52.270
that said, if we could build
a machine learning algorithm

00:24:52.270 --> 00:24:55.540
to analyze these volumes of data
that we're gathering everyday

00:24:55.540 --> 00:24:59.050
at the data centers, maybe we
could find some insights that

00:24:59.050 --> 00:25:03.030
would help us drive the energy
efficiency even further.

00:25:03.030 --> 00:25:04.390
That's exactly what he did.

00:25:04.390 --> 00:25:06.690
And then he partnered
with our DeepMind group

00:25:06.690 --> 00:25:10.380
to basically make it
production-worthy.

00:25:10.380 --> 00:25:12.150
We employed that.

00:25:12.150 --> 00:25:16.080
And now we have
the full ML system

00:25:16.080 --> 00:25:18.030
operating parts of
our cooling systems

00:25:18.030 --> 00:25:22.860
at the data center fully
autonomously, very safely.

00:25:22.860 --> 00:25:26.160
And of the energy
consumption for the cooling,

00:25:26.160 --> 00:25:29.220
we've achieved about a 30%
reduction in the energy

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:31.530
consumption for
cooling by having

00:25:31.530 --> 00:25:36.235
the ML system operate the data
center cooling system for us.

00:25:36.235 --> 00:25:37.860
The way it does that,
it's very simple.

00:25:37.860 --> 00:25:41.250
It's looking at about 19
to 20 independent variables

00:25:41.250 --> 00:25:42.360
in real time.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:46.260
And it's matching the cooling
needs to the data center

00:25:46.260 --> 00:25:48.040
load at that time.

00:25:48.040 --> 00:25:51.000
So it's doing it in real time,
and it's constantly adjusting.

00:25:51.000 --> 00:25:53.910
And this is basically
far more efficient

00:25:53.910 --> 00:25:55.450
than humans could do it.

00:25:55.450 --> 00:25:58.917
And so we developed it, we
tested it over many years.

00:25:58.917 --> 00:26:01.000
And little by little, we
made it fully autonomous.

00:26:01.000 --> 00:26:03.042
So now, it's controlling
the data center cooling.

00:26:09.120 --> 00:26:12.240
As I talked about, we
have a strong commitment

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:15.900
to powering the data
centers on renewable energy.

00:26:15.900 --> 00:26:21.360
So we started our renewable
energy and our zero carbon

00:26:21.360 --> 00:26:25.230
journey roughly
in about 2007 when

00:26:25.230 --> 00:26:29.790
we decided that as a company
we were going to achieve a zero

00:26:29.790 --> 00:26:31.560
carbon footprint.

00:26:31.560 --> 00:26:34.530
And we did achieve that in
2007, and we did that primarily

00:26:34.530 --> 00:26:38.260
by buying very high
quality carbon offsets.

00:26:38.260 --> 00:26:41.670
So we were just offsetting
our carbon footprint.

00:26:41.670 --> 00:26:43.710
And we did that for 10 years.

00:26:43.710 --> 00:26:48.150
And in 2017, apart from
celebrating the 10 year

00:26:48.150 --> 00:26:50.920
anniversary of being a
carbon neutral company,

00:26:50.920 --> 00:26:54.120
we also announced
that we had achieved

00:26:54.120 --> 00:26:55.990
another major milestone.

00:26:55.990 --> 00:27:01.380
And that was that we are now
buying enough renewable energy

00:27:01.380 --> 00:27:05.850
to offset the entire consumption
of energy at Google data

00:27:05.850 --> 00:27:10.830
centers and Google overall,
the entire enterprise.

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:12.460
What does that mean?

00:27:12.460 --> 00:27:14.880
That means that we are
the world's largest

00:27:14.880 --> 00:27:18.480
corporate purchaser
of renewable energy.

00:27:18.480 --> 00:27:21.300
We have since 2000--

00:27:21.300 --> 00:27:25.230
roughly 10-ish-- we have signed
over 30 large scale power

00:27:25.230 --> 00:27:28.750
purchase agreements for
solar and wind energy.

00:27:28.750 --> 00:27:33.280
That is over three
and 1/2 gigawatts

00:27:33.280 --> 00:27:35.330
of energy under contract.

00:27:35.330 --> 00:27:36.730
So we are now matching.

00:27:36.730 --> 00:27:39.820
For every kilowatt or
megawatt hour of energy

00:27:39.820 --> 00:27:42.220
we consume around
all of Google, we

00:27:42.220 --> 00:27:47.700
are now buying at least an equal
amount of renewable energy.

00:27:47.700 --> 00:27:50.320
And we are the first
company of our size

00:27:50.320 --> 00:27:54.280
to ever achieve 100%
matching of our consumption

00:27:54.280 --> 00:27:55.675
versus renewable.

00:27:58.632 --> 00:28:00.090
This is a little
bit of our journey

00:28:00.090 --> 00:28:02.295
when we started in 2007.

00:28:02.295 --> 00:28:04.170
Frankly, I should have
put on this slide when

00:28:04.170 --> 00:28:10.470
I was just saying all that
stuff, but anyhow, like I said,

00:28:10.470 --> 00:28:14.240
we now have over three and
1/2 gigawatts under contract.

00:28:14.240 --> 00:28:18.440
We've spent over $5 billion
on these energy purchase

00:28:18.440 --> 00:28:19.285
agreements.

00:28:23.380 --> 00:28:26.860
In 2017, as I mentioned,
we had 10 consecutive years

00:28:26.860 --> 00:28:28.210
of carbon neutrality.

00:28:28.210 --> 00:28:31.180
And we matched 100%
of our consumption.

00:28:31.180 --> 00:28:33.850
But as I talked about, we
don't rest on our laurels.

00:28:33.850 --> 00:28:36.460
And so even that is
not good enough for us.

00:28:36.460 --> 00:28:41.050
Because in order for us to
truly take responsibility

00:28:41.050 --> 00:28:45.010
for our energy
footprint and our impact

00:28:45.010 --> 00:28:47.380
to the environment
and the climate,

00:28:47.380 --> 00:28:49.300
we need to be able
to match our energy

00:28:49.300 --> 00:28:52.660
consumption with
renewable energy 24 hours

00:28:52.660 --> 00:28:55.570
a day, seven days a week.

00:28:55.570 --> 00:28:58.630
As you know, some of the
grids in which we operate

00:28:58.630 --> 00:29:01.840
don't have abundant
renewable energy on them.

00:29:01.840 --> 00:29:04.600
In some places, the
regulatory environment

00:29:04.600 --> 00:29:06.340
doesn't allow you to
go out and purchase

00:29:06.340 --> 00:29:09.213
your own renewable
energy contracts.

00:29:09.213 --> 00:29:10.630
So in order to
achieve that, we're

00:29:10.630 --> 00:29:13.910
going to have to come up
with some creative thinking.

00:29:13.910 --> 00:29:16.610
So the team has been doing that.

00:29:16.610 --> 00:29:19.930
And one of the
demonstrations of this

00:29:19.930 --> 00:29:23.170
is that in Taiwan, where
the regulatory environment

00:29:23.170 --> 00:29:26.890
previously had not allowed
corporate companies

00:29:26.890 --> 00:29:30.040
to go out and buy their
own energy contracts,

00:29:30.040 --> 00:29:32.330
and put it on the
grid there in Taiwan.

00:29:32.330 --> 00:29:35.500
So we worked with the Taiwan
power company and the Taiwan

00:29:35.500 --> 00:29:36.760
national government.

00:29:36.760 --> 00:29:39.360
And over a period of time
of collaborating with them,

00:29:39.360 --> 00:29:42.610
we were able to get the
Taiwan Power Act changed

00:29:42.610 --> 00:29:46.660
that allows us to buy
renewable energy contracts

00:29:46.660 --> 00:29:48.500
and put it on the
grid in Taiwan.

00:29:48.500 --> 00:29:51.500
So our first one that
we did is pretty unique.

00:29:51.500 --> 00:29:55.870
These are fish ponds
where they fish farm,

00:29:55.870 --> 00:29:58.030
they raise fish in
these ponds, right?

00:29:58.030 --> 00:30:01.720
So we have a developer that
is now building solar panels

00:30:01.720 --> 00:30:04.810
above these fish ponds.

00:30:04.810 --> 00:30:08.890
So now we have a solar pond, or
a solar farm above a fish farm.

00:30:08.890 --> 00:30:11.650
And it's right in
the same local area

00:30:11.650 --> 00:30:13.180
as where our data center is.

00:30:13.180 --> 00:30:17.320
So that is helping us to
green our operations in Taiwan

00:30:17.320 --> 00:30:21.130
and helping us basically
take more accountability

00:30:21.130 --> 00:30:23.780
for our consumption
in that local region.

00:30:23.780 --> 00:30:25.830
So this is the first
of a kind in Taiwan.

00:30:25.830 --> 00:30:27.850
And by the way, when we
make these agreements,

00:30:27.850 --> 00:30:29.560
we don't do it just for us.

00:30:29.560 --> 00:30:31.870
We make the policy
so that anyone

00:30:31.870 --> 00:30:36.190
that wants to follow our
actions can do that also.

00:30:36.190 --> 00:30:37.840
Another example.

00:30:37.840 --> 00:30:41.350
In Belgium, we did
this solar farm.

00:30:41.350 --> 00:30:43.840
This is about
10,000 solar panels

00:30:43.840 --> 00:30:45.610
on our data center property.

00:30:45.610 --> 00:30:47.500
Normally, we would
do these off-site

00:30:47.500 --> 00:30:49.630
where we could have
larger installations.

00:30:49.630 --> 00:30:51.820
But in this case,
this was a great way

00:30:51.820 --> 00:30:56.800
to match or offset our energy
consumption for our office

00:30:56.800 --> 00:30:58.090
areas and our facilities.

00:30:58.090 --> 00:31:01.750
Not the data center itself,
that would be too large.

00:31:01.750 --> 00:31:05.140
However, in another part in
the southeast of the United

00:31:05.140 --> 00:31:07.540
States in Tennessee
and Alabama, we

00:31:07.540 --> 00:31:10.390
worked with the Tennessee
Valley Authority

00:31:10.390 --> 00:31:14.260
to build one of the largest
PPAs is that has ever

00:31:14.260 --> 00:31:16.000
been done in that region.

00:31:16.000 --> 00:31:22.530
That's 1.5 million solar
panels, 413 megawatts

00:31:22.530 --> 00:31:24.030
that are being
built specifically

00:31:24.030 --> 00:31:29.010
for Google that will offset
our consumption in that region

00:31:29.010 --> 00:31:30.240
for the foreseeable future.

00:31:33.300 --> 00:31:37.820
So we're going to have to
work hard to actually make

00:31:37.820 --> 00:31:42.020
this a reality, 24 by
7 renewable energy.

00:31:42.020 --> 00:31:44.720
As you know, like I
said, renewable energy

00:31:44.720 --> 00:31:47.280
is not available
everywhere in the world.

00:31:47.280 --> 00:31:50.460
It's not available every day
or every hour of the day.

00:31:50.460 --> 00:31:52.520
The sun doesn't
shine 24 hours a day.

00:31:52.520 --> 00:31:55.530
The wind doesn't
blow 24 hours a day.

00:31:55.530 --> 00:31:57.900
So we're going to have to
get very creative with this.

00:31:57.900 --> 00:31:59.780
We've developed a
tool that allows

00:31:59.780 --> 00:32:03.230
us to analyze at a
data center level

00:32:03.230 --> 00:32:04.850
the consumption
of the data center

00:32:04.850 --> 00:32:08.840
and match it to what the
renewable energy generation is,

00:32:08.840 --> 00:32:11.780
hour-for-hour, so that we
can see how we're doing.

00:32:11.780 --> 00:32:14.480
In some data centers in
parts of the world where

00:32:14.480 --> 00:32:17.160
we don't have a lot of
access to renewable energy,

00:32:17.160 --> 00:32:19.040
it's not matched very well yet.

00:32:19.040 --> 00:32:23.460
In other parts, we're over
65% matching today already.

00:32:23.460 --> 00:32:26.630
So we have a long
way to go, but we

00:32:26.630 --> 00:32:29.930
think it's important enough to
dedicate a team of resources

00:32:29.930 --> 00:32:31.460
to this initiative.

00:32:31.460 --> 00:32:32.837
And it's a long-term goal.

00:32:32.837 --> 00:32:34.670
I don't know when we're
going to achieve it.

00:32:34.670 --> 00:32:36.560
It is arguably a moonshot.

00:32:36.560 --> 00:32:38.960
Typical Google thinking.

00:32:38.960 --> 00:32:41.450
But in order to really
take accountability

00:32:41.450 --> 00:32:43.820
for our footprint, this
is what we have to do.

00:32:47.800 --> 00:32:50.970
As I mentioned, this happens
to be the data center

00:32:50.970 --> 00:32:55.860
in North Carolina showing how
on a day, hour-by-hour basis,

00:32:55.860 --> 00:32:59.160
we're matching with
our renewable energy.

00:32:59.160 --> 00:33:02.070
So you can see that in
some parts of the year,

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:04.350
we are 82% carbon-free.

00:33:04.350 --> 00:33:07.290
Other parts of the year,
we're 49% carbon-free.

00:33:07.290 --> 00:33:11.040
So it changes, of course,
seasonally, and hour by hour.

00:33:11.040 --> 00:33:15.032
But this is giving us the
insights that we need.

00:33:15.032 --> 00:33:16.490
The really cool
thing that we did--

00:33:16.490 --> 00:33:19.310
remember that ML
system I told you

00:33:19.310 --> 00:33:22.640
about that was operating our
data center cooling systems?

00:33:22.640 --> 00:33:27.890
Well, DeepMind and us partnered
together to adjust that system

00:33:27.890 --> 00:33:30.740
and said, what if we apply
that same type of machine

00:33:30.740 --> 00:33:33.110
learning to a wind farm.

00:33:33.110 --> 00:33:34.950
What would that do?

00:33:34.950 --> 00:33:39.440
Well, we developed
jointly a new ML system

00:33:39.440 --> 00:33:44.930
that allows us to predict what
the energy generation of a wind

00:33:44.930 --> 00:33:49.112
farm will be 36
hours in advance.

00:33:49.112 --> 00:33:50.320
Why is that really important?

00:33:50.320 --> 00:33:52.270
Because if you're
a grid operator,

00:33:52.270 --> 00:33:55.150
the intermittency of
renewables make it really hard

00:33:55.150 --> 00:33:57.783
for you to plan how much
generation you need.

00:33:57.783 --> 00:33:59.200
You know what your
demand is going

00:33:59.200 --> 00:34:00.575
to be more or less
because you've

00:34:00.575 --> 00:34:03.460
got a long history of
looking at your demand

00:34:03.460 --> 00:34:04.600
from your customers.

00:34:04.600 --> 00:34:07.540
But you don't know what the
output of that wind farm

00:34:07.540 --> 00:34:09.590
is going to be tomorrow.

00:34:09.590 --> 00:34:11.949
Well, this ML system
allows us to predict it

00:34:11.949 --> 00:34:14.449
with a high degree of
fidelity what it is.

00:34:14.449 --> 00:34:16.449
So now they can plan
on that and put that

00:34:16.449 --> 00:34:18.800
into their baseload
calculations.

00:34:18.800 --> 00:34:22.719
We can better utilize the full
output of the wind farm, which

00:34:22.719 --> 00:34:24.699
has resulted-- now
this is the early days,

00:34:24.699 --> 00:34:27.070
I don't know if this is
going to be how it is always,

00:34:27.070 --> 00:34:28.389
I hope it's better.

00:34:28.389 --> 00:34:31.120
But we've already achieved
about a 20% improvement

00:34:31.120 --> 00:34:33.340
in the value of the
wind farm that this

00:34:33.340 --> 00:34:36.790
was done at, the value
of that renewable energy.

00:34:36.790 --> 00:34:39.730
So I think there's
a lot of hope here.

00:34:43.409 --> 00:34:46.320
And the third pillar is
the circular economy.

00:34:46.320 --> 00:34:50.880
And our belief is that we should
reuse and recycle and have

00:34:50.880 --> 00:34:54.330
a zero waste to landfill goal.

00:34:54.330 --> 00:34:56.489
We have achieved zero
waste to landfill

00:34:56.489 --> 00:35:00.070
at some of our data centers,
but not all of them.

00:35:00.070 --> 00:35:03.210
Today, across the
entire portfolio

00:35:03.210 --> 00:35:07.650
we're 91% diversion
of waste to landfill.

00:35:07.650 --> 00:35:12.820
That means that only
9% of all of our waste,

00:35:12.820 --> 00:35:15.510
whether it's from
construction or operations,

00:35:15.510 --> 00:35:17.260
is making it to a landfill.

00:35:17.260 --> 00:35:19.890
This required us to think
about things differently,

00:35:19.890 --> 00:35:22.620
to come up with better
reuse strategies

00:35:22.620 --> 00:35:24.390
so that we can take
components that maybe

00:35:24.390 --> 00:35:28.360
aren't used for that
particular customer,

00:35:28.360 --> 00:35:30.420
but use them for another
internal customer.

00:35:30.420 --> 00:35:31.890
Like for instance,
if Search said

00:35:31.890 --> 00:35:34.890
those machines are
out of date for us,

00:35:34.890 --> 00:35:39.390
but some of our other internal
services can still use them.

00:35:39.390 --> 00:35:42.360
So rather than
just recycle them,

00:35:42.360 --> 00:35:43.890
we're reusing a lot of that.

00:35:43.890 --> 00:35:46.050
We're remanufacturing
a lot of our servers

00:35:46.050 --> 00:35:48.570
with recycled components.

00:35:48.570 --> 00:35:51.300
And we're reselling
some of our components

00:35:51.300 --> 00:35:53.490
as well so that we're
really diverting

00:35:53.490 --> 00:35:56.310
a lot of that otherwise
would be electronic waste.

00:36:00.530 --> 00:36:02.150
And it's not just
us talking about it,

00:36:02.150 --> 00:36:06.710
but a lot of other people have
noticed our sustainability

00:36:06.710 --> 00:36:07.760
efforts.

00:36:07.760 --> 00:36:15.530
So we've been at the top of the
CDP awards list for many years.

00:36:15.530 --> 00:36:20.270
We were given the Zayed Future
Energy Award in Abu Dhabi

00:36:20.270 --> 00:36:22.290
a couple of years ago.

00:36:22.290 --> 00:36:25.700
So it's being noticed.

00:36:25.700 --> 00:36:27.938
It's not just we're
doing it for ourselves,

00:36:27.938 --> 00:36:30.230
but we're doing it for all
of you, all of our customers

00:36:30.230 --> 00:36:34.400
as well, because now you know
that the workloads that you run

00:36:34.400 --> 00:36:39.110
in Google Cloud are helping you
achieve your own sustainability

00:36:39.110 --> 00:36:42.268
and your own carbon
neutrality initiatives.

00:36:47.640 --> 00:36:51.050
This is a quote here from
one of our customers, Etsy,

00:36:51.050 --> 00:36:55.400
their CTO said that underneath
their ecological goal

00:36:55.400 --> 00:37:00.440
they want to be 100%
renewable electricity by 2020.

00:37:00.440 --> 00:37:02.450
And that they know that
all of their workloads

00:37:02.450 --> 00:37:05.150
that they're processing
with us on Google Cloud

00:37:05.150 --> 00:37:08.060
are helping them to achieve
this initiative because they

00:37:08.060 --> 00:37:12.040
are already being powered by
renewable energy on our cloud.

00:37:18.310 --> 00:37:22.120
So if we review our
core principles,

00:37:22.120 --> 00:37:24.730
our core principles for
our data center program

00:37:24.730 --> 00:37:29.660
are around performance,
availability, reliability,

00:37:29.660 --> 00:37:34.300
security, and an
environmental responsibility,

00:37:34.300 --> 00:37:38.080
and a commitment to
100% sustainability.

00:37:38.080 --> 00:37:43.960
We truly are building the
world's most sustainable cloud.

00:37:43.960 --> 00:37:48.810
[MUSIC PLAYING]

